Interfaces — with default and static methods.

An interface is a pure contract: a named set of methods that a type promises to provide, with no state of its own. Where an abstract class says "you are a kind of thing", an interface says "you can do this thing" — a capability. It's the most important tool in Java for keeping code loosely coupled.

Types sign the contract with the implements keyword. So a shape interface might declare an area method and a perimeter method — any shape must be able to give both. A circle then implements shape, storing a radius and providing area as pi times radius squared, and perimeter as two pi radius. Because circle implements shape, it promises both methods. And now any code that needs "something with an area" can simply accept a shape, and never care which concrete class it actually got. It's the same dynamic dispatch as before, but with no shared base class forced onto the implementers.

Here's the superpower. A class extends only one superclass, but it can implement many interfaces. That's how a type mixes in several unrelated capabilities. A circle might implement shape, and comparable, and serializable, all at once. So it is a shape, it can be compared, and it can be serialized — three orthogonal capabilities, with no awkward single hierarchy. Interfaces are Java's answer to "I need to be several things at once."

Originally, interfaces held only signatures — no bodies. But since Java eight, an interface can ship a default method: a real implementation that every implementer inherits, unless it chooses to override it. So the shape interface could add a default describe method that returns "area equals", then the area. Every implementer gets that for free. This solved a genuinely hard problem: you can add a method to a widely-used interface without breaking the thousands of classes that already implement it, because they simply inherit the default. That's exactly how the list interface gained stream and sort, without a flag day that broke everyone's code.

An interface can also hold static methods — utilities that belong to the concept, but aren't tied to any one instance, and are called on the interface itself. For example, a static factory on the shape interface might hand you a unit circle.

So here's the design habit to build. Program to interfaces, not to concrete classes. Accept a list, not an array list; a shape, not a circle. Your code then depends on the capability, not the implementation — so you can swap implementations freely, test with fakes, and extend the system with new types. This "depend on abstractions" instinct is the through-line of good object-oriented design. And it sets up the final question of this module: when should you combine objects by inheriting, versus by composing?
